{"content_hash": "b4dc9b0e6aebb5b8", "version_id": 303, "article_id": 31, "level_code": "en_b1", "lang": "en", "level": 3, "level_label": "B1 中级", "title": "Nvidia Bets on Physical AI for Safer Robots and Robotaxis", "title_zh": "英伟达押注物理AI，让机器人和无人出租车更安全", "has_translation": true, "topic": "tech", "source": "arstechnica", "source_url": "https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/10/nvidias-big-bet-on-physical-ai-aims-for-safer-robotaxis-humanoid-robots/", "image": {"type": "photo", "url": "https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1451187580459-43490279c0fa?ixid=M3wxMDk3Mzk1fDB8MXxzZWFyY2h8MXx8dGVjaG5vbG9neSUyMGNvbXB1dGVyfGVufDB8MHx8fDE3OTE1NTAyOTd8MA&ixlib=rb-4.1.0&w=1600&q=75&fm=jpg&fit=max", "provider": "unsplash", "credit": "Photo by NASA on Unsplash", "author": "NASA", "credit_url": "https://unsplash.com/@nasa?utm_source=daily-english-news&utm_medium=referral", "source": "Unsplash", "source_url": "https://unsplash.com/photos/photo-of-outer-space-Q1p7bh3SHj8?utm_source=daily-english-news&utm_medium=referral", "fallback": {"type": "gradient", "from": "hsl(228, 42%, 62%)", "to": "hsl(266, 46%, 48%)", "label": "TECH", "seed": "cb64368c7dd7"}}, "published_date": "2026-10-08", "word_count": 268, "reading_minutes": 1.3, "lead": "Nvidia is spending billions on physical AI, which means robots and self-driving cars. Its new Halos system helps these machines work safely around people.", "lead_zh": "英伟达正投入数十亿美元发展物理AI，也就是机器人和自动驾驶汽车。它新推出的Halos系统能帮助这些机器安全地在人身边工作。", "preview": "Nvidia is famous for AI chips used in data centers, but it has also invested billions in physical AI. This includes robotics and self-drivin", "preview_zh": "英伟达以数据中心里的AI芯片闻名，但它也在物理AI上投入了数十亿美元。这包括机器人和自动驾驶汽车。该公司打造了一套全栈安全系统，让机器能避免伤害附近的人。英伟达在2025年推出了用于自动驾驶汽车的Halos系统，然后在2026年6月将其扩展到机器人领域。", "has_audio": true, "audio": {"id": 293, "url": "https://pub-aba43a6fb1db4dc08fede1dbc81f3241.r2.dev/audio/31/en_b1.mp3", "duration": 114.562, "size_bytes": 691056, "engine": "edge-tts", "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural", "word_count": 297, "has_timeline": true}, "detail_url": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/chjam12301-sys/news-etl@bb5aacc/content/data/versions/303.json", "paragraphs": ["Nvidia is famous for AI chips used in data centers, but it has also invested billions in physical AI. This includes robotics and self-driving cars. The company created a full-stack safety system so machines can avoid harming people nearby. Nvidia launched its Halos system in 2025 for self-driving cars, and then expanded it to robotics in June 2026.", "The Halos system includes hardware and software. The Nvidia IGX Thor computing module has a special processor for safety tasks. The Halos operating system watches every hardware block and software library to find failures quickly. It also includes simulation tools for testing robots in virtual environments, and a sensor bridge that can spot corrupted data.", "Adapting Halos from cars to robots was difficult because safety means different things. A robot vacuum cleaning a hallway has different safety needs than a forklift moving heavy loads. Nvidia had to build a flexible platform that lets developers create custom safety functions without breaking the basic system.", "Agility Robotics was the first to use Halos in its Digit 5 humanoid robot. The robot can now work safely near people without external sensors or barriers. Boston Dynamics is also an early partner, and its Spot, Stretch, and Atlas robots will use the safety platform. Other companies like KION Group and LG are also working with Halos.", "Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang said physical AI already brings nearly $10 billion in yearly revenue. However, it is still unclear if humanoid robots can become general-purpose workers. The push for more robots in the US, China, and Europe is growing, and safety is the key to making it work."], "paragraphs_zh": ["英伟达以数据中心里的AI芯片闻名，但它也在物理AI上投入了数十亿美元。这包括机器人和自动驾驶汽车。该公司打造了一套全栈安全系统，让机器能避免伤害附近的人。英伟达在2025年推出了用于自动驾驶汽车的Halos系统，然后在2026年6月将其扩展到机器人领域。", "Halos系统包括硬件和软件。英伟达IGX Thor计算模块有一个专门处理安全任务的处理器。Halos操作系统会监控每一个硬件模块和软件库，以便快速发现故障。它还包括在虚拟环境中测试机器人的模拟工具，以及一个能发现损坏数据的传感器桥接器。", "把Halos从汽车应用到机器人上很困难，因为安全对不同机器意味着不同的东西。一个在走廊打扫的机器人吸尘器，和一个在仓库搬运重物的叉车，安全需求完全不同。英伟达必须打造一个灵活的平台，让开发者能创建自定义安全功能，同时不破坏基础系统。", "敏捷机器人公司是第一家在其Digit 5人形机器人中使用Halos的公司。现在这个机器人可以在人身边安全工作，不需要外部传感器或屏障。波士顿动力公司也是早期合作伙伴，它的Spot、Stretch和Atlas机器人将使用这个安全平台。KION集团和LG等其他公司也在与Halos合作。", "英伟达CEO黄仁勋表示，物理AI已经带来近100亿美元的年收入。然而，人形机器人能否成为通用工人仍不确定。美国、中国和欧洲正在推动更多机器人，而安全是让这一切成功的关键。"], "body": "Nvidia is famous for AI chips used in data centers, but it has also invested billions in physical AI. This includes robotics and self-driving cars. The company created a full-stack safety system so machines can avoid harming people nearby. Nvidia launched its Halos system in 2025 for self-driving cars, and then expanded it to robotics in June 2026.\n\nThe Halos system includes hardware and software. The Nvidia IGX Thor computing module has a special processor for safety tasks. The Halos operating system watches every hardware block and software library to find failures quickly. It also includes simulation tools for testing robots in virtual environments, and a sensor bridge that can spot corrupted data.\n\nAdapting Halos from cars to robots was difficult because safety means different things. A robot vacuum cleaning a hallway has different safety needs than a forklift moving heavy loads. Nvidia had to build a flexible platform that lets developers create custom safety functions without breaking the basic system.\n\nAgility Robotics was the first to use Halos in its Digit 5 humanoid robot. The robot can now work safely near people without external sensors or barriers. Boston Dynamics is also an early partner, and its Spot, Stretch, and Atlas robots will use the safety platform. Other companies like KION Group and LG are also working with Halos.\n\nNvidia CEO Jensen Huang said physical AI already brings nearly $10 billion in yearly revenue. However, it is still unclear if humanoid robots can become general-purpose workers. The push for more robots in the US, China, and Europe is growing, and safety is the key to making it work.", "body_zh": "英伟达以数据中心里的AI芯片闻名，但它也在物理AI上投入了数十亿美元。这包括机器人和自动驾驶汽车。该公司打造了一套全栈安全系统，让机器能避免伤害附近的人。英伟达在2025年推出了用于自动驾驶汽车的Halos系统，然后在2026年6月将其扩展到机器人领域。\n\nHalos系统包括硬件和软件。英伟达IGX Thor计算模块有一个专门处理安全任务的处理器。Halos操作系统会监控每一个硬件模块和软件库，以便快速发现故障。它还包括在虚拟环境中测试机器人的模拟工具，以及一个能发现损坏数据的传感器桥接器。\n\n把Halos从汽车应用到机器人上很困难，因为安全对不同机器意味着不同的东西。一个在走廊打扫的机器人吸尘器，和一个在仓库搬运重物的叉车，安全需求完全不同。英伟达必须打造一个灵活的平台，让开发者能创建自定义安全功能，同时不破坏基础系统。\n\n敏捷机器人公司是第一家在其Digit 5人形机器人中使用Halos的公司。现在这个机器人可以在人身边安全工作，不需要外部传感器或屏障。波士顿动力公司也是早期合作伙伴，它的Spot、Stretch和Atlas机器人将使用这个安全平台。KION集团和LG等其他公司也在与Halos合作。\n\n英伟达CEO黄仁勋表示，物理AI已经带来近100亿美元的年收入。然而，人形机器人能否成为通用工人仍不确定。美国、中国和欧洲正在推动更多机器人，而安全是让这一切成功的关键。", "text": "Nvidia is famous for AI chips used in data centers, but it has also invested billions in physical AI. This includes robotics and self-driving cars. The company created a full-stack safety system so machines can avoid harming people nearby. Nvidia launched its Halos system in 2025 for self-driving cars, and then expanded it to robotics in June 2026. The Halos system includes hardware and software. The Nvidia IGX Thor computing module has a special processor for safety tasks. The Halos operating system watches every hardware block and software library to find failures quickly. It also includes simulation tools for testing robots in virtual environments, and a sensor bridge that can spot corrupted data. Adapting Halos from cars to robots was difficult because safety means different things. A robot vacuum cleaning a hallway has different safety needs than a forklift moving heavy loads. Nvidia had to build a flexible platform that lets developers create custom safety functions without breaking the basic system. Agility Robotics was the first to use Halos in its Digit 5 humanoid robot. The robot can now work safely near people without external sensors or barriers. Boston Dynamics is also an early partner, and its Spot, Stretch, and Atlas robots will use the safety platform. Other companies like KION Group and LG are also working with Halos. Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang said physical AI already brings nearly $10 billion in yearly revenue. However, it is still unclear if humanoid robots can become general-purpose workers. The push for more robots in the US, China, and Europe is growing, and safety is the key to making it work.", "vocab": [{"word": "physical AI", "pos": "noun", "zh": "物理AI", "note": "指在物理世界中行动的AI，比如机器人和自动驾驶汽车"}, {"word": "full-stack", "pos": "adj", "zh": "全栈的", "note": "指包含所有必要部分的完整系统"}, {"word": "module", "pos": "noun", "zh": "模块", "note": "硬件或软件的一个独立部分"}, {"word": "processor", "pos": "noun", "zh": "处理器", "note": "计算机中处理数据的核心部件"}, {"word": "simulation", "pos": "noun", "zh": "模拟", "note": "在虚拟环境中模仿真实情况"}, {"word": "corrupted", "pos": "adj", "zh": "损坏的", "note": "数据出错或无法正常读取"}, {"word": "adapt", "pos": "verb", "zh": "改编，调整", "note": "为了新用途而改变"}, {"word": "custom", "pos": "adj", "zh": "定制的", "note": "根据特定需求制作的"}, {"word": "humanoid", "pos": "adj/noun", "zh": "人形的；人形机器人", "note": "外形像人的机器人"}, {"word": "general-purpose", "pos": "adj", "zh": "通用的", "note": "能用于多种不同任务的"}], "title_original": "Nvidia's big bet on physical AI aims for safer robotaxis, humanoid robots", "summary_original": "Full-stack safety solution for physical AI is being used by robotics companies.", "body_original": "The physical AI boom?\nNvidia’s big bet on physical AI aims for safer robotaxis, humanoid robots\nFull-stack safety solution for physical AI is being used by robotics companies.\nJeremy Hsu\n–\nOct 8, 2026 7:15 am\n|\n7\nAgility Robotics' Digit humanoid robot uses Nvidia's Halos system to help safely work around people.\nCredit:\nAgility Robotics\nAgility Robotics' Digit humanoid robot uses Nvidia's Halos system to help safely work around people.\nCredit:\nAgility Robotics\nText\nsettings\nStory text\nSize\nSmall\nStandard\nLarge\nWidth\n*\nStandard\nWide\nLinks\nStandard\nOrange\n* Subscribers only\nLearn more\nMinimize to nav\nInvestors bullish on AI data centers may be fueling Nvidia’s multitrillion-dollar market capitalization, but the AI chipmaker has also bet billions of dollars on physical AI technologies such as robotics and self-driving cars. Part of that gambit has involved developing a full-stack safety system that companies can build upon to reduce the risk of their machines harming nearby people.\nNvidia’s Halos system\nlaunched in 2025\nwith hardware and software tools to help developers implement guardrails in self-driving cars and other autonomous vehicles. Then the company expanded its safety architecture to more physical AI technologies by announcing\nNvidia Halos for Robotics\nin June 2026—with the goal of enabling safe deployments of autonomous mobile robots in warehouses, humanoid robots walking around inside a factory, or even surgical robots.\n“Now the AI models are getting capable, the robot hardware is getting capable, and a thing we thought is going to be the next bottleneck is safety,”\nAmit Goel\n, head of robotics ecosystem and edge computing at Nvidia, told Ars. “So that’s why we launched our Halos for Robotics to unlock the capability of these systems.”\nThe expanded safety offering for robotics comes as Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang described the physical AI business as already driving nearly $10 billion in annual revenue for the company during an\nappearance on the All-In Podcast\nin March 2026. Huang previously began highlighting physical AI as Nvidia’s\nsecond-most important growth category\nin 2025.\nExpanding safety from AVs to robots\nSo what does Nvidia’s full-stack safety system involve? It starts with hardware, such as the\nNvidia IGX Thor computing module\nfor robotics and industrial applications that has an independent processor dedicated to safety-related workloads. “In the context of physical AI, you need to have your functional system and your safety system all running on the same silicon,” Goel said.\nOn the software side, the Halos operating system was designed to enable constant monitoring of “every hardware block” and “every software library” to swiftly spot any failures, Goel explained. The system also isolates safety-critical computing workloads to avoid any potential interference.\nThe system also includes the Nvidia Holoscan Sensor Bridge that connects sensor data with safety-related computer processing in a way that can easily identify corrupted data. This can be embedded in individual hardware components, like a microcontroller or Field Programmable Gate Array.\nLast but not least, the Halos package also includes Nvidia’s simulations for testing robots in virtual environments, and an inspection lab program that allows robotics companies and other partners to get quick feedback on any safety “artifacts” that arise during robotic development.\nHowever, adapting Nvidia Halos from autonomous vehicles to robotics required accommodating many different definitions of safety. The definition of functional safety for autonomous driving generally remains the same across different automotive companies and countries, Goel explained. But a robotic vacuum cleaning a hallway will have very different safety considerations compared to a robotic forklift handling heavy payloads in a warehouse loading dock.\n“We had to reimagine and do a lot of foundational work to build a platform that is programmable, that gives all the hooks to the developers to actually define custom safety functions, but at the same time not break the underlying infrastructure that we built,” Goel told Ars. “Because giving flexibility can come at the cost of losing some control over the stack.”\nCustom safety functions are also necessary because robots may operate in complex, diverse environments.\n“If you are a robot coming down an aisle at six miles per hour, your senses do not see what are the blind spots and what is coming around the corner,” Gold said. “What the robots do is almost come to a halt before the turn, because they really don’t know what’s happening in all these factories, warehouses and hospitals—they’re unstructured environments and things can come from anywhere.”\nReal-world robot deployments\nThe US startup Agility Robotics was first to incorporate Nvidia Halos into its latest\nDigit 5 humanoid robot, which is designed to work safely near people\nwithout requiring isolated workstations or physical barriers. The Nvidia system helped Agility bring all the relevant safety sensors and hardware inside the robot, whereas previous Digit robot operations have also relied on external sensors placed around the robot’s work cell to ensure safety.\n“Now the robot is literally unchained—the safety goes with the robot wherever it goes,” Goel told Ars. “Now they can operate everywhere in a factory or a warehouse without having to set up a brand new infrastructure every time they want to do a new task.”\nThe Massachusetts-based robotics company Boston Dynamics has also been an\nearly partner in the Nvidia Halos safety accreditation program\n, with a focus on building a safety platform that encompasses their Spot robot dogs, wheeled Stretch robots with a large robotic arm, and Atlas humanoid robots that could start\ndeploying in the automotive factories\nof parent company Hyundai by 2028.\nOther companies working to incorporate Nvidia Halos include the German company KION Group that deploys self-driving forklifts and other autonomous mobile robots, along with the South Korean company LG that is\ndeveloping its own humanoid robot\nbased on Nvidia’s IAI foundation model for humanoid robots called Isaac GROOT.\nIt remains to be seen if\nhumanoid robots\ncan become\nautonomous, general-purpose robot workers\ncapable of handling practically any human task. But the latest push by the United States, China, and Europe to deploy more robots extends well beyond humanoids—and is putting robots to the test in many new environments.\n“Earlier robots were mostly confined in one station doing one thing,” Goel said. “But in the new age of robotics with a generalized body as well as generalized skills, it is essential that the robots can do multiple tasks in multiple environments—and the only way you can do that in production is if you improve safety.”\nJeremy Hsu\nTech Reporter\nJeremy Hsu\nTech Reporter\nJeremy Hsu is a reporter exploring a wide range of topics across deep tech and AI. He has previously written for New Scientist, Scientific American, IEEE Spectrum, Wired, Undark Magazine and MIT Tech Review, among many other publications, about topics such as deepfakes, data centers, drones, battery tech, robotics, and GPS jamming. He also has a Master of Arts in Journalism from NYU, and a bachelor's degree from University of Pennsylvania in History and Sociology of Science, with a minor in English.\n7 Comments", "fetched_at": "2026-10-08T12:45:01.060343", "timeline": [{"i": 0, "w": "Nvidia", "s": 0.1, "e": 0.713, "sm": 100, "em": 712, "cs": 0, "ce": 6, "si": 0}, {"i": 1, "w": "is", "s": 0.725, "e": 0.9, "sm": 725, "em": 899, "cs": 7, "ce": 9, "si": 0}, {"i": 2, "w": "famous", "s": 0.912, "e": 1.325, "sm": 912, "em": 1325, "cs": 10, "ce": 16, "si": 0}, {"i": 3, "w": "for", "s": 1.337, "e": 1.562, "sm": 1337, "em": 1562, "cs": 17, "ce": 20, "si": 0}, {"i": 4, "w": "AI", "s": 1.575, "e": 1.913, "sm": 1575, "em": 1912, "cs": 21, "ce": 23, "si": 0}, {"i": 5, "w": "chips", "s": 1.925, "e": 2.337, "sm": 1925, "em": 2337, "cs": 24, "ce": 29, "si": 0}, {"i": 6, "w": "used", "s": 2.35, "e": 2.65, "sm": 2350, "em": 2650, "cs": 30, "ce": 34, "si": 0}, {"i": 7, "w": "in", "s": 2.663, "e": 2.75, "sm": 2662, "em": 2750, "cs": 35, "ce": 37, "si": 0}, {"i": 8, "w": "data", "s": 2.763, "e": 3.038, "sm": 2762, "em": 3037, "cs": 38, "ce": 42, "si": 0}, {"i": 9, "w": "centers", "s": 3.05, "e": 3.6, "sm": 3050, "em": 3599, "cs": 43, "ce": 50, "si": 0}, {"i": 10, "w": ",", "s": 3.6, "e": 3.938, "sm": 3599, "em": 3937, "cs": 50, "ce": 51, "si": 0}, {"i": 11, "w": "but", "s": 3.938, "e": 4.075, "sm": 3937, "em": 4075, "cs"